Restore the shutdown lock in PeriodicExportingMetricReader::OnShutDown.

MetricReader::Shutdown stores shutdown_ and then OnShutDown notifies cv_ and
joins the worker. Because the notify does not hold cv_m_, nothing orders it
against the worker's predicate evaluation in DoBackgroundWork, which does hold
cv_m_. When the store+notify land between the predicate returning false and the
worker atomically releasing cv_m_ to block, the notification is lost and the
worker sleeps out its full remaining export interval. join() waits that out, so
shutdown stalls for one export interval (up to 60s for the deployed tiers, and
effectively forever for tests configured with a long interval).

This is a straight restoration of upstream commit 4f32bc6f (PR #2553, merged
2024-05-08), which was reverted incidentally by commit 605c3e8e (PR #2584,
"[SDK] Fix forceflush may wait for ever", 2024-05-27). The removal is unrelated
to that PR's own fix: OnShutDown uses cv_m_/cv_, while the force-flush path
uses the separate force_flush_m_/force_flush_cv_ pair. Still absent upstream as
of v1.28.0 and main.

--- a/sdk/src/metrics/export/periodic_exporting_metric_reader.cc
+++ b/sdk/src/metrics/export/periodic_exporting_metric_reader.cc
@@ -285,7 +285,11 @@ bool PeriodicExportingMetricReader::OnShutDown(std::chrono::microseconds timeout
 {
   if (worker_thread_.joinable())
   {
-    cv_.notify_all();
+    {
+      // ensure that `cv_` is awaiting, and the update doesn't get lost
+      std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(cv_m_);
+      cv_.notify_all();
+    }
     worker_thread_.join();
   }
   return exporter_->Shutdown(timeout);
